A widget may declare rectangles inside it at their own depth, in steps of
the person's element limit (_q_stereo_depth_rects, a list of rectangles each
with a depth from -1 to 1 of one step, queue row 121): a document viewer's
desk one step behind the window and its pages at the window's plane. The
window is then painted once per eye while the compositor shows depth inside
it, whatever style draws it, each rectangle moved by its own share by the
rule widgets are moved by, and the strip a nearer rectangle's shift uncovers
continues what lies beneath it, so its edge keeps its depth (o121-0002).

The window learns from the compositor whether it is awake and the person's
element limit (version 7 of kde_stereo_content_v1, the element_limit event,
which Stereo KWin +stereo3d69 sends); with an older compositor the declared
rectangles are drawn flat and the log says why, once per window (o121-0001).

Every Qt program's print preview (QPrintPreviewWidget) declares its desk one
step down and its paper at the window's plane, its shadows on the desk
(o121-0003). Without the compositor's word, on a 2D screen, in a window lying
behind the active one or with the element limit at 0, everything is drawn as
before.
